From: gavin_darkglider <rbrown4014@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 22:27:14 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 07/13] docs: document rotation support and the 1.6.0 changes

Describe splash=rotate:N, how themes are picked on rotated consoles, and
the default silent tty (tty16, the docs still said tty8).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013zuE5eXGVUDWEu4YZj7gYa
---
 core/NEWS                   | 26 +++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 core/docs/kernel_parameters | 19 +++++++++++++++++--
 2 files changed, 42 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/core/NEWS b/core/NEWS
index bb7d2d6..d3e4dca 100644
--- a/core/NEWS
+++ b/core/NEWS
@@ -1 +1,25 @@
-# Stub NEWS file to satisfy automake.
+splashutils 1.6.0
+=================
+
+Revival release: builds on current toolchains and works with the 6.x
+fbcondecor kernel patch.
+
+* Rotated consoles: the splash follows the framebuffer console rotation
+  (fbcon=rotate:N or the fbcon sysfs knobs).  Themes are chosen for the
+  resolution as seen on screen and the silent splash is drawn rotated;
+  verbose pictures are rotated by the kernel.  New splash=rotate:N option
+  to override the detected rotation.
+* Bundled 2007 copies of zlib, libpng, libjpeg and freetype and the klibc
+  build are gone.  The kernel helper is linked statically against the
+  system libraries (pkg-config --static); --disable-static-helper links it
+  dynamically and splash_geninitramfs then copies its libraries.
+* splash_geninitramfs --append works with any initramfs compression and
+  with multi-segment images by appending a separate archive.
+* The kernel helper now switches VTs through /dev/tty0, so the silent
+  splash also works with a serial console listed last.
+* OpenRC plugin builds against current OpenRC, starts fbsplashd without a
+  shell, and no longer uses its service list after freeing it.
+* Many bug fixes: a format string bug in the fifo writer, terminal flags
+  wiped on silent tty cleanup, box blend-in/out never fading, UTF-8
+  decoding past the end of strings, fbcondecor_ctl always exiting 0, and
+  more (see the git history).
diff --git a/core/docs/kernel_parameters b/core/docs/kernel_parameters
index ed3840a..4ea9728 100644
--- a/core/docs/kernel_parameters
+++ b/core/docs/kernel_parameters
@@ -20,8 +20,13 @@ a continuous configuration string. The following options are recognized:
  - fadein     - Use a 'fade from black' effect to display the silent image.
  - fadeout    - Use a 'fade to black' effect after the bootup is finished.
  - theme:foo  - Use theme 'foo'.
- - tty:n      - Sse the n-th tty as the silent tty. Default silent tty is
-                tty8.
+ - tty:n      - Use the n-th tty as the silent tty. Default silent tty is
+                tty16.
+ - rotate:n   - Draw the splash rotated by n * 90 degrees clockwise
+                (0-3, same values as fbcon=rotate:n).  By default the
+                splash follows the framebuffer console's rotation, taken
+                from /sys/class/graphics/fbcon/rotate or fbcon=rotate:n,
+                so this is only needed to override it.
  - kdgraphics - Run in KDGRAPHICS mode. Use of this option is discouraged
                 but might be necessary when some kernel drivers try to
                 display messages on the foreground tty despite the 'quiet'
@@ -42,6 +47,16 @@ A sample full kernel command line:
   root=/dev/hda5 video=vesafb:1024x768-32@85
   splash=silent,fadein,theme:linuxrocks,tty:12 console=tty1
 
+Rotated consoles
+----------------
+
+On a rotated console (fbcon=rotate:1 or 3, e.g. a portrait panel used in
+landscape orientation) themes are picked for the resolution as seen on the
+screen: a 720x1280 panel rotated by 90 degrees uses the theme's 1280x720.cfg.
+Pictures are designed upright as usual; splashutils and the kernel take
+care of laying them out.  This needs a kernel with the rotation-aware
+fbcondecor patch for the verbose (fbcondecor) part.
+
 Please note that the default splash mode is 'off'. You have to explicitly
 specify 'silent' or 'verbose' on the kernel command line in order to get
 the splash working.
